%I A401483 #8 Oct 31 2026 12:42:11
%S A401483 1,16,81,512,625,1296,2401,10000,14641,19683,28561,38416,41472,50625,
%T A401483 65536,83521,130321,194481,234256,279841,314928,320000,456976,707281,
%U A401483 810000,923521,1185921,1229312,1336336,1500625,1874161,1953125,2085136,2313441,2825761,3111696,3418801
%N A401483 Powerful numbers whose prime factorization exponents are all squares.
%C A401483 Numbers whose prime factorization exponents are all squares > 1.
%C A401483 All the terms are 4-full (A036967).
%C A401483 All the terms of A197680 are a product of a term in this sequence and a coprime squarefree number (A005117).
%C A401483 The asymptotic density of terms in A197680 whose powerful part (A057521) is a term k in this sequence is (1/(zeta(2)*k)) * Product_{p|k} p/(p+1) = 1/(zeta(2)*A001615(k)). Therefore, A357016 (the asymptotic density of A197680) can be evaluated by summing over terms in this sequence (see the 2nd formula).
%H A401483 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A401483/b401483.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401483 <a href="/index/Pow#powerful">Index entries for sequences related to powerful numbers</a>.
%F A401483 Sum_{n>=1} 1/a(n) = Product_{p prime} (1 + Sum_{k>=2} 1/p^(k^2-1)) = 1.079979933858337473571... .
%F A401483 Sum_{n>=1} 1/A001615(a(n)) = zeta(2) * A357016.
%t A401483 q[k_] := k == 1 || AllTrue[FactorInteger[k][[;; , 2]], # > 1 && IntegerQ[Sqrt[#]] &]; Select[Range[170000], q]
%o A401483 (PARI) isok(k) = {my(e = factor(k)[, 2]); for(i = 1, #e, if(e[i] == 1 || !issquare(e[i]), return(0))); 1;}
%Y A401483 Intersection of A001694 (or A036967) and A197680.
%Y A401483 Cf. A001615, A005117, A013661, A057521, A357016.
%Y A401483 Similar sequences: A335988, A369567, A401482.
%K A401483 nonn,easy,new
%O A401483 1,2
%A A401483 _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 27 2026
